WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.105
Although approaches vary, many hunts follow a similar pattern.

00:00:03.105 --> 00:00:07.285
First, the hunter develops a hypothesis based on threat intelligence, observed trends,

00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:11.111
adversary behaviors, organizational concerns, or previous investigations.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:11.360
Next,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.030
the hunter identifies relevant data sources and examines them to test the hypothesis.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:20.085
The search may start broad and then narrow as evidence, context, and patterns emerge.

00:00:20.085 --> 00:00:23.055
The hunter collects evidence, refines the investigation,

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:27.035
and determines whether observed activity is benign, suspicious, or malicious.

00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:29.990
Evidence shapes the conclusion.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.055
A hunt should be able to explain why the activity does or does not support the hypothesis.

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:34.780
If malicious activity is identified,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.010
the findings transition into incident response and remediation.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:42.000
If no threat is found, the organization still benefits by validating assumptions,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.840
identifying visibility gaps, and improving future detections.
